1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,752
A merchant may run several websites, so which one is allowed to take payments?

2
00:00:04,802 --> 00:00:09,960
A sales point is one website, with the return addresses where buyers come back after paying.

3
00:00:10,010 --> 00:00:17,448
Every return address must be an https page on the website's own domain, so a payment can never send a buyer somewhere else.

4
00:00:17,498 --> 00:00:22,392
Each sales point can carry the website's logo, which buyers see on the payment page.

5
00:00:22,442 --> 00:00:32,088
The logo goes to the same file store, in its own bucket: PNG or JPEG, up to one megabyte, and a new logo replaces the old file.

6
00:00:32,138 --> 00:00:40,752
A new sales point starts as a draft, and can't take payments yet; its webhook is explained in episode nineteen, and its limits in episode twenty-three.

7
00:00:40,802 --> 00:00:43,752
An admin checks the website and records the result.

8
00:00:43,802 --> 00:00:47,688
A failed check must list what is wrong, and every check is kept.

9
00:00:47,738 --> 00:00:56,040
The deal sets the card schemes and the refund window, and a longer window needs a reason and stays under a configured maximum.

10
00:00:56,090 --> 00:01:01,296
Switching it on needs an active merchant, a passed latest check, and at least one card scheme.

11
00:01:01,346 --> 00:01:04,488
Once it is live, the return addresses are frozen.

12
00:01:04,538 --> 00:01:09,456
A live website can be suspended and restored, and a closed one stays closed.

13
00:01:09,506 --> 00:01:15,768
Other modules ask one question: may this sales point take a payment right now, and the answer fails closed.

14
00:01:15,818 --> 00:01:20,088
Next, the platform libraries that every module and service shares.
